# Java/Kotlin + Spring patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
Spring Boot 3.x design and its common anti-patterns, for both Java and
Kotlin. Applies only to `*.java`/`*.kt`/`*.kts` files.

## Transaction boundaries and the self-invocation pitfall

- `@Transactional` is placed on the outermost service method that defines
  the unit of work, not scattered on every repository call.
- Spring's declarative transactions are proxy-based (AOP): a call from
  one method to another `@Transactional` method **on the same class**,
  through `this`, bypasses the proxy entirely — the annotation is
  silently ignored, no new transaction starts and no rollback happens on
  that inner call. Split the self-invoking method out into a separate
  bean the caller depends on (constructor-injected), so the call crosses
  a real proxy boundary, instead of calling `this.otherTransactionalMethod()`.
- Keep a `@Transactional` method free of unrelated I/O (HTTP calls,
  file access) that would hold the transaction/connection open longer
  than necessary.

## Spring Data JPA query patterns

- Watch for N+1: iterating a collection and accessing a lazy association
  per item issues one query per item. Fix with `@EntityGraph` on the
  repository method, a JPQL `JOIN FETCH`, or a projection that selects
  only the fields actually needed — do not switch every association to
  `FetchType.EAGER` as a blanket fix, which just moves the N+1 into every
  unrelated query that loads the entity.
- Prefer a derived query method or `@Query` with named parameters over
  building JPQL/native SQL by string concatenation.
- Use a projection (an interface or DTO) for a read path that only needs
  a subset of an entity's fields, instead of loading the full entity
  graph and mapping it down in the service layer.

## Dependency injection and bean design

- Define an interface at the consumer boundary (the package/module that
  calls through it) when a component genuinely has more than one
  implementation or needs to be mocked across module boundaries; do not
  add an interface with a single Spring-managed implementation "for
  testability" when constructor injection with a concrete class already
  gives a test the same seam via a test double.
- Favor constructor injection producing an immutable, fully-initialized
  bean over setter injection or field injection, which both allow a bean
  to exist in a partially-configured state.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A "god service" that accumulates unrelated business logic across
  multiple domains — split by bounded responsibility, mirroring the
  controller/service/repository layering above.
- Catching a broad `Exception`/`RuntimeException` in a service method
  only to log and swallow it, instead of letting it propagate to a
  `@ControllerAdvice`/`@ExceptionHandler` that maps it to the right HTTP
  response.
- A `@RestController` method containing business/query logic directly
  instead of delegating to a service — controllers should stay thin.
